		},
		{
			"x": -81.07148949522066,
			"y": 11662.877785122493,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:TwUkmu8kYt5s46-xY_JCY",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1747.630067387855,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "code"
										}
									],
									"text": "Summary"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": ": We have the ctr command line utility that comes with containerd and works with containerd, which is used for debugging purpose only and has a very limited set of features. So ideally you wouldn't be using this at all. So you can ignore this . Then we have the nerdctl CLI, which is again from the containerd community. But this is a docker like CLI for containerd, used for general purpose to create containers and supports the same or more features than docker CLI. So this is something that we will be using a lot more going forward. "
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Then we have crictl utility which is from the Kubernetes community, the mainly used to interact with CRI compatible runtime. So it's not just containerd. This can be used for all CRI supported runtimes and this is mainly used for debugging purposes."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
