			"parentId": "page:f68luzkeSOzKLbQERhXxs",
			"index": "b0iJAQ",
			"typeName": "shape"
		},
		{
			"x": -13.328125,
			"y": 2990.3671875,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:UQj0aJoH0NY5q8WqUlyHm",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1449.79296875,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "YAML (stands for YAML Ain't Markup Language) is a human readable data serialization language. It is most commonly used to write configuration files for tools like Kubernetes, Docker compose, Ansible, and Github Actions. YAML was first created in 2001. "
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Think of it as a cleaner, easier-to-read alternatives to JSON or XML. Instead of using complex brackets, tags, or braces, YAML relies strictly on indentation(spaces) to structure data."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Key Rules of YAML"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "orderedList",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto",
								"start": 1,
								"type": null
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "listItem",
									"attrs": {
										"dir": "auto"
									},
									"content": [
										{
											"type": "paragraph",
											"attrs": {
												"dir": "auto"
											},
											"content": [
												{
													"type": "text",
													"text": "Spaces, Not Tabs: you must use regular spaces ( usually 2 or 4) to indent your code. Using a Tab key will break the file."
												}
											]
										}
									]
								},
								{
									"type": "listItem",
									"attrs": {
										"dir": "auto"
									},
									"content": [
										{
											"type": "paragraph",
											"attrs": {
												"dir": "auto"
											},
											"content": [
												{
													"type": "text",
													"text": "Case Sensitivity: containers and Containers are treated as completely different things."
												}
											]
										}
									]
								},
								{
									"type": "listItem",
									"attrs": {
										"dir": "auto"
									},
									"content": [
										{
											"type": "paragraph",
											"attrs": {
												"dir": "auto"
											},
											"content": [
												{
													"type": "text",
													"text": "Key-value Pairs: Data is written as a key followed by a colon and a space(key:value)."
												}
											]
										}
									]
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "For example: here is a quick comparision of a sample data in three formats where we display a list of servers and their information."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
			"parentId": "page:f68luzkeSOzKLbQERhXxs",
			"index": "axAGA",
			"typeName": "shape"
		},
		{
			"x": -18.706615075484493,
			"y": 4255.550462804772,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:a-K2ROwAMSpIQr2Bw1vdz",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1484.20703125,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "External Communication."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Suppose we deployed our pods having a web application running on it. How do we as an external user access the web page?. First of all let’s look at the existing setup. The Kubernetes node has an IP address and that is 192.168.1.2 My laptop is on the same network as well, so it has an IP address 192.168.1.1 The Internal Pod network is in the range 10.244.0.0, and the pod has an IP 10.244.0.2 clearly I cannot ping or access the pod at address 244.0.2 as it’s in a separate network. So what are the options to see the web page?. First if we were to SSH into the Kubernetes node at 192.168.1.2 from node, we would be able to access the pods web page by doing a curl. Or if the node has a GUI we would fire up a browser and see the web page in a browser following the http://10.244.0.2 But this is from inside the Kubernetes node, and that’s not what I really want. I want to be able to access the web server from my own laptop, without having to ssh into the node, and simply by accessing the IP of the Kubernetes node. So we need something in the middle to help us map requests to the node from our laptop, through the node to the pod running the web container. This is where the Kubernetes service comes into play. The Kubernetes Service is an object just like pods, replica sets, or deployments that we worked with before. One of its use cases is to listen to a port on the node, and forward requests on that port to a port on the pod running the web application. This type of service is know as Nodeport severice."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
			"parentId": "page:JovrcF97X_PRHRO9ZdSRz",
			"index": "b0cAyz",
			"typeName": "shape"
		},
