				"size": "s",
				"w": 1416.55078125,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "suppose we deployed three instances of our front end web application and it has three pods. we would like to create a replication controller or replicaset to ensure that we have threee active pods at any time. and yes this is one of the use case of replica sets. you can use it to monitor existing pods if you have them already craeted as it is in this example. In case they were not created, the replica set will create them for you The role of replica set is to monitor the pods and if any of them were to fail, deploy new ones. The replica set is in fact a process that monitors the pods. Now, how does replica set know what pods to monitor?. There could be hundereds of other pods in the cluster running different applications. This is where labeling our pods during creation comes in handy. we could now provide labels as a filter for replica set under the selector section, we use the match labels filter and provide the same label that we used while creating the pods. This way, the replica set knows which pods to monitor. The same concept of labels and selectors is used in many other places throughout kubernets."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
			"parentId": "page:f68luzkeSOzKLbQERhXxs",
