		{
			"x": -2.9253975380361226,
			"y": 12346.158590545725,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:FXnxrPr90BF_mWPt2Dj0i",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1474.71875,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Suppose we started with three replicas and in the future we decided to scale to six. How do we update our replica set to scale to six replicas?. Well there are multiple ways to do it. The first is to update the number of replicas in the defination file to six, then run the "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "code"
										}
									],
									"text": "kubectl apply -f replicaset-defination.yml "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "to specify the same file using the f parameter, and that will update the replica set to have six replicas. The second way to do it is to run the "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "code"
										}
									],
									"text": "kubectl scale —replicas=6 -f replicaset-defination.yml "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "use the replicas parameter to provide the new number of replicas, and specify the same file as input. You may either input the defination file or provide the replica set name in the type name format. However remember that using the file name as input will not result in the number of replicas being updated automatically in the file."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
			"parentId": "page:f68luzkeSOzKLbQERhXxs",
			"index": "b2suPM",
			"typeName": "shape"
